@@ -391,6 +391,12 @@ and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0
 
 ### Fixed
 
+- **Hook-layer git-environment leak sweep** ([#1691](https://github.com/modu-ai/moai-adk/issues/1691), card t560, follow-up to card t516) — git exports `GIT_DIR` and `GIT_INDEX_FILE` into every hook it runs, and **those outrank the working directory**, so neither `cmd.Dir` nor `git -C <dir>` confines a child process: it resolves through the caller's git context instead of the directory it was given. Card t516 closed this at `runStep` in `internal/hook/quality/gate.go`; it did not close **the sibling call one screen away in the same file and the same process**. `stagedFiles` read the *caller's* staged set, so under a pre-commit hook the quality gate chose which steps to run from another repository's state — silently, because neither branch errors. The scrub list has moved to the new `internal/gitenv` package precisely because a repair confined to one package could not reach its siblings; `internal/hook/quality` now delegates to it, and t516's existing tests bind unchanged, which is what makes the move readable as a move.
+  - **Scrubbed (6 sites)**: `quality/gate.go` `stagedFiles`, `agentmemory.go` `gitOutput`, `navigator_detect.go` `changedAtForProject`, `session_end.go` `getModifiedGoFiles`, `security/guardian.go` `runGit`, `worktree_create.go` `resolveWorktreeRepoRoot`. Scope stays narrow: only repository **location** is removed. Identity (`GIT_AUTHOR_*`, `GIT_COMMITTER_*`) and behaviour (`GIT_EDITOR`, `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, …) reach the child, because a child stripped of its identity fails for an unrelated reason and one stripped of its behaviour settings does something different rather than doing it somewhere different.
+  - **Reachability is stated, not implied**: only `stagedFiles` is confirmed reachable from a git-hook environment (the pre-commit hook shells out to `moai gate`). The other five have Claude Code as their parent, which does not export `GIT_DIR` — their change **closes a leak surface and does not repair an observed incident**, and is not claimed as one.
+  - **Deliberately NOT scrubbed (5 sites)**: `worktree_base_branch.go`. Its discriminant compares `git rev-parse --git-dir` against `--git-common-dir` to decide whether this is the primary checkout, so removing `GIT_DIR` would change its answer — scrubbing there would *be* the defect rather than fix one. The file is unchanged and pinned by `worktree_base_branch_ambient_env_test.go`, which asserts the two `GIT_DIR` cases must **differ**; under a mutation that adds the scrub they collapse to the same answer and the test fails, so the guard was shown to fail rather than merely to exist.
+  - **Reported, not repaired (1 site)**: `quality/tool_registry.go` `RunTool` is defect-shaped — arbitrary child, `cmd.Dir` set, `cmd.Env` nil — but its only callers are `Formatter` and `Linter`, which have no non-test constructor outside the package (control: `runStep` 8 hits vs those constructors 0 hits outside the package). Repairing dead code produces a change no test can verify.
+  - **Defect 1 of #1691 (`core.bare` flips to `true`) is now VERIFIED**, where t516 recorded it as unreproduced. That earlier non-reproduction was correct as an observation and too weak as a conclusion: its model used a host repository with **no linked worktree**, so `GIT_DIR` named a plain `.git`. Measured on git 2.50.1 (Apple Git-155), a child `git init` flips the host's `core.bare` **only** when `GIT_DIR` names a per-worktree gitdir (`<host>/.git/worktrees/<wt>`); the shared gitdir and `GIT_INDEX_FILE` alone do not. The key lands in `<host>/.git/config`, which every linked worktree shares, so one test fixture makes the whole repository fail with `fatal: this operation must be run in a work tree`. Reproduced through production code by mutating away the t516 scrub (`core.bare: "false" before, "true" after`), and the discriminator itself is pinned as a test so a future git that changes this fails loudly instead of hollowing the guard out. **"Did not reproduce" and "the firing shape has not been measured yet" produce the same output.**
 - **[SPEC-CODEX-DISABLE-EXIT-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-CODEX-DISABLE-EXIT-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t548, Tier S). **A guard refusal from `moai skills disable <name> --codex` now exits non-zero instead of 0, so a script can branch on a refused request instead of parsing stdout text.** The verb's `Skipped:` branch — the five reasons a guard declines the write (no path key, an unencodable character, duplicate entries, an unrecognized line, no rewritable key) — printed its reason and returned nil, so by exit code a refused request was indistinguishable from a performed disable. The exit mattered beyond cosmetics: two of the refusal reasons are fixed by `moai clean --codex-skills`, and exit 0 hid that handoff from any caller branching on the status. The `Skipped:` report still prints — the human reads the reason; only the exit contract changes. The rest of the outcome map is pinned unchanged: Unchanged (the desired state already holds) stays 0, absent inputs (no project mirror, an unresolvable Codex home, an absent or unreadable config) stay 0 — the fail-open doctrine that a missing input is not an error is untouched — and name-resolution failures stay non-zero. The per-class contract is now stated on the verb's `--help` (`performed 0 / refused 1 / absent 0`) and in the runner's doc comment, so the bucketing the SPEC found undocumented is documented rather than left to branch reading. The Skipped-exits-non-zero test was observed RED on the pre-change tree before the one-line fix landed, with per-branch companions (Unchanged exits 0, unresolved-home fails open) so each branch of the contract carries its own guard. Write surface: `internal/cli/codex_skills_disable.go` (one branch return + the contract doc comment), `internal/cli/skills.go` (`--help` exit-code table), `internal/cli/codex_skills_disable_test.go` (+3 tests). 7 acceptance criteria (AC-CDE-001..007), 7 PASS / 0 FAIL; the Skipped branch's new exit is the deliberate M2 operator adjudication (Outcome B over the document-only Outcome A, recorded in `progress.md` §E.1). This sync commit carries the 3-phase close (`spec.md` frontmatter `in-progress → completed`, `status` + `updated` only), the `progress.md` §E.4 signal, and this entry; `sync_commit_sha` is written as the `pending-backfill-sync` placeholder in this commit — a commit cannot cite its own hash — and the resolved value is backfilled in a following commit.
 - **[SPEC-CODEX-BLANK-REVIEW-FAILCLOSED-001](.moai/specs/SPEC-CODEX-BLANK-REVIEW-FAILCLOSED-001/spec.md)** — sync-phase close (3-phase plan→run→sync, card t551, Tier M, GitHub #1632 blank-output axis). **A codex review whose body carries no non-whitespace character no longer reports a `pass` it never produced: the blank body is now classified as absent and returns `inconclusive` with a summary naming blankness as the cause.** The gap this closes: the codex review-text path tested emptiness by exact equality (`== ""`) at three sites, so a body of whitespace counted as content, reached `synthesizeReviewOutput`, and was turned into `pass` by native review mode's documented default for an unrecognized body — a review that never reached a verdict laundered into one that found nothing wrong. The three sites are one repair rather than three, because repairing the guard alone would have made things worse in a second direction: `bestCodexReviewText` prefers the structured `exitedReviewMode.review` over the free-form `agentMessage.text` whenever the former is non-empty by exact equality, so a blank structured review **shadows** a real agent message, and a guard-only fix would convert that case — one that has genuine review content available — from a wrong `pass` into an equally wrong `inconclusive`. All four call sites now route through one named discriminator `codexReviewTextIsBlank` (`internal/cli/mcp_codex.go:367`, `strings.TrimSpace(s) == ""`): collection at `:1184` / `:1187` (the `awaitCodexTurnReview` loop **reassigns** on every matching item, so under exact equality a later blank item could overwrite an earlier real one), selection at `:1308` (`bestCodexReviewText`), and the guard at `:855` (`runTurn`). The discriminator is a named function rather than four inline comparisons because the defect is shaped exactly like the miss-one hazard that produced it — three sites written with the same test, a fourth added later, and one body passing all four.
   - **The verdict is `inconclusive`, not `fail`, and the SPEC's `FAILCLOSED` name refers to closing the loophole rather than to adopting a blocking verdict.** `codex` is an OPTIONAL backend; a blank body is one symptom of a backend that is present but not answering, and blocking a session on that would contradict the optional-backend contract. **Backend-absent behavior is unchanged** — an unavailable codex still fails open to `inconclusive` by design, and only blankness was reclassified. The two states stay distinguishable on purpose: the blank-output summary is `codex review output was blank: no verdict text was produced`, deliberately carrying **no** `codex unavailable: ` prefix, so a reader can tell "the backend was not there" from "the backend answered without saying anything". A new factory `inconclusiveReviewWithSummary` takes the summary verbatim; the existing `inconclusiveReview` keeps the prefix and its behavior.
